Procedure-centric approach – Each legacy procedure may be mapped to a corresponding service, resulting in a model closely aligned with existing procedures defined in NGAP [6], particularly when the procedure is atomic (e.g., Error Indication, NG Reset, etc.). Pros: direct traceability with the existing protocol specification, allowing a relatively straightforward transition from the current procedure-based model, resulting in a lower migration effort. Cons: definition of a large number of narrowly scoped services, potentially resulting in duplicated semantics across services and making it more difficult to introduce richer interaction patterns such as query-based or subscription-based mechanisms. Functional service classes – Services are identified according to higher-level functional capabilities rather than individual procedures. A service may encompass multiple related operations associated with a given functional capability exposed across the RAN–CN boundary. Pros: reduction of service fragmentation, allowing clearer identification of service producers, service consumers, and data ownership following typical service-based interaction models. Cons: Need for mapping identified services to legacy NGAP procedures, as the correspondence is not necessarily one-to-one. The procedure-centric approach can be considered as a transitional option to facilitate migration from legacy NGAP-based interactions, while the functional service class approach may represent a target architecture (e.g., for new services) aligned with service-based principles, consistent with the approach specified in 5G CN [4], where services are defined as functional capabilities decoupled from underlying signalling procedures and may include multiple operations. The main characteristics of the two approaches are summarized in Table 1. Table 1 – Impact
