{"chunk_id":"R3-162196-003","collection":"ran3_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["Qualcomm"],"tdocNumber":"R3-162196","title":"Granularity and reconfiguration of the functional split"},"point_id":"13292188000954284668"}
This paper has discussed gNB split aspects and specifically the split granularity and possible reconfiguration. While further analysis on the splits themselves would be useful, we can capture already possible granularity options as in section 2. The observations made are as follows: On Granularity: at least node level granularity should be supported (CU/DU). Further granularity (e.g. per UE) seems useful but requires analysis based on QOS handling assumptions. In this case, one instance of the interface may need to support different split levels. Configuration of the split (or range of splits, including split configuration) may rely on O&M, or may be negotiated over the interface (this aspect requires further study). On Reconfiguration: two types of potential reconfiguration scenarios can be identified (mobility and traffic triggered reconfigurations).

{"chunk_id":"R3-253494-003","collection":"ran3_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["Huawei"],"tdocNumber":"R3-253494","title":"Discussion on UE performance feedback for AI/ML-based Mobility Optimization in NR-DC"},"point_id":"18332902770135735329"}
id for all UE performance metrics, it was highlighted in contribution [10] that, in case of NR-DC with split bearers, measurements performed in the gNB-DU (at RLC level) are per leg and hence they cannot be aggregated to derive per-DRB measurements (even though it is possible to collect the individual MCG and SCG leg level measurements), due to the fact that there is no common RLC SDU point for MCG and SCG legs and neither MN nor SN knows the transmission/buffer status of data in the other leg. The issue may be solved by relying on PDCP-level measurements performed by the node hosting the PDCP entity (either MN or SN), which allow to collect per-DRB information in all NR-DC scenarios regardless of the bearer type (MCG, SCG, or split). Therefore, and as a general principle, the MN requests the SN to report UE performance only if there is at least a SN-terminated bearer configured at SN Addition or MN-initiated SN Change. Observation 1: PDCP-level measurements performed by the node hosting the PDCP entity (either MN or SN) allow to collect per-DRB information in all NR-DC scenarios regardless of the bearer type (MCG, SCG, or split). Proposal 2: RAN3 to agree the general principle that MN requests the SN to report UE performance only if there is at least a SN-terminated bearer configured for the UE at SN Addition or MN-initiated SN Change. Based on the above, we suggest waiting for the outcome of the discussion on UE performance measurements in split architecture – with related SA5 feedback on feasibility of measurements at PDCP, as requested in [9] – and strive to find commonalities with the corresponding discussions in case of AI/ML-based mobility optimization in NR-DC. Such consideration is in line with the FFS from the last meeting [4]: Proposal 3: RAN3 to wait for the outcome of the discussion on UE performance measurements in split architecture and strive to find commonalities with the corresponding discussions in case of NR-DC. An important issue to discuss is the granularity of the UE performance metrics that the SN needs to report. Our understanding is that UE performance metrics in TS 28.558 [8] are measured on a per-DRB per-UE granularity, other than the Average UE Throughput DL/UL measurements which are already defined with a per-UE granularity. However, the UE performance metrics in clause 9.2.3.179 of TS 38.423 [7] are intended to be reported on a per-UE granularity. As a starting point, we can consider UE performance to be reported on a per-UE granularity as done in Rel-18. This because the intended use of such UE performance should be to evaluate whether the AI/ML model decision to setup NR-DC with a certain SN for the UE was beneficial with respect to keeping the concerned UE in single connectivity. And single connectivity is addressed in Rel-18 where only per-UE UE performance are reported. To derive per-UE UE performance metrics (Average Packet Delay DL/UL and Average Packet Loss DL) starting from the corresponding per-DRB per-UE measurements the RAN3 common understanding from RAN3#124 meeting can be considered, that is, per-UE UE performance is derived as the average value of per-DRB measurements of the UE performance metrics [11]: Proposal 4: RAN3 to agree that, as a starting point, UE performance is reported from the SN to the MN on a per-UE granularity.

